<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">El Dorado</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">El Dorado, 'the golden one', was a Spanish legend of a native ruler covered in gold dust, which grew into the myth of a golden city or kingdom in the interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. It arose from the Muisca rite at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-guatavita">Lake Guatavita</a> in which a new chief offered gold to the waters. It drove many costly expeditions, including those of the Welsers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-raleigh">Walter Raleigh</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/el-dorado.jpg" alt="El Dorado" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Camilo.Errante · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laguna_de_Guatavita_centro_de_la_leyenda_del_Dorado.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> in 1492, saw golden ornaments worn by some inhabitants and became convinced that the lands held great wealth. European accounts drew on older legends of rich utopias across the Atlantic, such as the Isles of the Blessed and the Isle of Seven Cities. Ambrosius Dalfinger, a governor of Venezuela employed by the Welser banking family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, looted golden trinkets around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-maracaibo">Lake Maracaibo</a> from 1529 and was told they came from a tribe in the mountains.</p><p>No written reference to a place or person called El Dorado has been found before 1541, apart from a statement by Gonzalo de la Peña in testimony of July 1539. In 1541 the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a> recorded a story current among the Spaniards of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> about a native ruler who went about covered in gold dust and washed it off each night. He did not say where this ruler lived. By the 1580s the story was firmly tied to the Muisca of the Colombian highlands, whose territory <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-jimenez-de-quesada">Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada</a> had conquered in 1537.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-pizarro">Gonzalo Pizarro</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> in February 1541 to find the golden king's country, with Francisco Orellana as second-in-command, and had local people tortured and killed when they could not guide him. In 1550 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> suspended all expeditions for nearly a decade. In 1560 Pedro de Ursúa set out down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> with 370 Spaniards; on 1 January 1561 Lope de Aguirre led a mutiny that killed Ursúa, and the search was abandoned.</p><p>Antonio de Berrio, bound by his father-in-law Quesada's will to keep searching, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco</a> in 1584 and again in 1587, and heard of a great lake called Manoa in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guianas">Guianas</a>. In March 1595 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-raleigh">Sir Walter Raleigh</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>, captured Berrio at San José on 7 April and announced his own plan to find the golden city. Reports of a lake called Parime kept it on 17th-century maps.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> crossing the area in 1674 found no trace of Lake Parime. In 1800 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-von-humboldt">Alexander von Humboldt</a> reported that the tribes used the word for any large body of water and suggested that seasonal flooding around Lake Amucu lay behind the legend. After Robert Schomburgk visited Lake Amucu in 1836 the lake vanished from maps. El Dorado stayed in European culture as a symbol of fabulous wealth, appearing as an idealised society in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>'s Candide of 1759 and in Werner Herzog's 1972 film Aguirre, the Wrath of God. Objects tied to the tradition, including the Muisca raft, are in the Museo del Oro in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “El Dorado” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A small gold raft found in a cave at Pasca in 1969 shows a Muisca chief and his attendants afloat, the kind of lake ceremony behind the legend of <b>El Dorado</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/muisca-confederation">Muisca Confederation · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Welser governors used the colony mainly as a base for expeditions into the interior in search of gold and the legendary <b>El Dorado</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/klein-venedig">Klein-Venedig</a></li><li><blockquote>Columbus sighted Trinidad in 1498, but Spain made no lasting settlement until 1592, when Antonio de Berrío founded San José de Oruña as a base for searching for <b>El Dorado</b> on the Orinoco.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-trinidad">Spanish Trinidad</a></li></ul></section>
